Title: Low Water
Credit: an original test screenplay, written for this studio
Note: not part of any other story

INT. BOATHOUSE - DAWN

Grey light through salt-streaked boards. NORA (58), oilskin over a nightdress, ties a rope to an iron ring. She has tied it ten thousand times. She does not look at her hands.

TOMAS (31), her son, in the doorway, boots unlaced, a mug in each hand.

TOMAS
You're early.

NORA
The boat was knocking.

He looks at the boat. It sits on mud. There is no water under it.

TOMAS
(quiet)
Mum. There's nothing to knock against.

Nora stops tying. She puts her palm flat on the hull, the way you would on a sleeping animal.

Three slow knocks, from inside the wood.

EXT. JETTY - CONTINUOUS

Tomas walks the jetty backwards, looking at the boathouse. Where the lake should be: black mud to the far trees, and a line of posts he has never seen before, leading out.

On the nearest post, at the height of a child, a brass hook. On the hook, wet and swinging, a small yellow coat.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
We lost the water in one night. It had been holding something down.

Tomas reaches for the coat. He does not touch it.

TOMAS
That's mine.

NORA (O.S.)
(from the boathouse, calm)
I know, love. Leave it on its hook.

SOUND: the knocking again. Not from the boat now. From the posts. Each one, nearer.

Tomas turns. The last post has a coat on it too. It is Nora's oilskin.

CUT TO BLACK.

Two full seconds of silence.
